// ---------- Long-form guide (paragraph strings may contain inline HTML) ----------

const guide = [
  {
    id: "who-we-are",
    heading: "Who we are and how remote delivery works for US clients",
    paragraphs: [
      `BtechWaleTech is three engineers in India. Ankur Kumar is a full stack developer. Santosh Sharma works on AI and machine learning, AWS, data science, technical SEO and deployment. Vedansh Shrivastava runs project management alongside data science, AI and automation work. That is the whole team. When you send a message, one of us reads it, and the person who replies is one of the people who will build your project. There is no sales layer, no account manager relaying notes, and no junior developer quietly assigned after the pitch call.`,
      `We have no office in the United States, no US entity, no mailing address in Delaware and no local staff. Plenty of offshore vendors blur that line with a rented address and a US phone number that forwards overseas. We would rather say it plainly: every US project is delivered remotely from India, and the process is designed so that distance does not cost you anything that matters. You can read more <a href="/about/">about the team</a> before deciding anything.`,
      `<strong>How a remote project actually runs.</strong> It starts with a call in your morning, which is our evening, plus a short written questionnaire about goals, audience, pages, integrations and deadlines. We send a written scope and an itemized quote. Once you approve it, a private staging link goes up early in the build and stays live until launch, so you review real pages on your own devices instead of static mockups. You get a written update at a fixed cadence, usually twice a week on larger builds and weekly on small ones, covering what shipped, what is next, anything blocked and anything we need from you.`,
      `<strong>Why the time difference helps.</strong> Most feedback arrives from US clients in the afternoon. We work through it during our day, which is your night, and a build with your changes applied is usually waiting when you open your laptop the next morning. That overnight cycle suits projects with a lot of review rounds, and it works because everything important is written down: decisions, open questions, scope changes and blockers. Verbal-only agreements fall apart across a ten-hour gap, so we keep a written trail for everything that matters.`,
      `<strong>Tools you already use.</strong> We work over email, WhatsApp, Slack, Google Meet, Zoom or Microsoft Teams, whichever your team prefers, and we can work inside your GitHub or GitLab organization, your Jira or Linear board, and your existing cloud accounts. Nothing is hosted in an account we control unless you explicitly ask us to manage it.`,
      `<strong>When we are the wrong choice.</strong> If your project needs someone physically present in your office several times a month, a US-based contracting entity for a government bid, or a large team on call around the clock, a local agency is the better fit, and we will tell you so in the first conversation. If it needs a site or application built well, launched properly and looked after, the distance is a scheduling detail.`,
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "services-detail",
    heading: "Services for the US market, in detail",
    paragraphs: [
      `<strong>Web development.</strong> Most US marketing sites we build are static or statically generated pages served from a CDN, which keeps them fast on both coasts, cheap to host and hard to hack. A static site of up to 100 pages starts from ${P.site}. When your marketing team needs to publish without us, we connect a headless CMS or build on WordPress with a lean theme instead of a heavy page builder. Forms route to the tools you already use, such as HubSpot, Salesforce, Pipedrive, a shared inbox or a Slack channel, with spam protection and a clear confirmation message. The <a href="/services/web-development/">web development service page</a> describes the stack in more depth.`,
      `<strong>Website design.</strong> US buyers compare quickly. A visitor arriving from a Google search usually decides on the first screen whether you look like the right fit, so we design around what they need to see first: what you do, where you do it, proof that you are real, and one clear next step. For a service business that means a visible phone number and a short quote form; for SaaS it means the product, the price and a sign-up; for a store it means the product, shipping terms and returns. Layouts are designed mobile first, because a large share of local searches happen on a phone, and typography and color are chosen for readability and contrast rather than fashion.`,
      `<strong>Ecommerce.</strong> Stores on Shopify, WooCommerce or a custom stack, starting from ${P.shop}, with Stripe, Shopify Payments, PayPal or Square at checkout. The ecommerce section below covers platform choice, payments, shipping and state sales tax in detail.`,
      `<strong>SEO for Google in the US.</strong> National and local rankings are won on different pages. We handle technical SEO, on-page work, content and local search, and set up hreflang properly when you serve Spanish-speaking customers or sell into Canada as well. Monthly SEO starts from ${P.seo}; the <a href="/services/seo-services/">SEO services page</a> lists the full scope, and the SEO section below explains our approach for the US market.`,
      `<strong>Custom software and web apps.</strong> Custom web applications start from ${P.app}. Typical US requests include customer portals, booking and scheduling systems, quoting and estimating tools, multi-tenant SaaS MVPs with Stripe billing, admin dashboards on top of an existing database, and integrations between tools that do not talk to each other, such as a CRM, QuickBooks and a field service app. We host on AWS or a comparable provider in a US region, in your account, and document the setup so another developer can take over later if you choose.`,
      `<strong>AI automation.</strong> AI automation starts from ${P.ai}. Useful projects are usually specific: reading incoming PDFs and invoices into structured data, drafting first replies to support tickets for a person to approve, qualifying inbound leads against your criteria, summarizing call notes into the CRM, or assembling a weekly report from several data sources. We keep a person in the loop wherever a mistake would cost money or trust, log what the system did, and do not send sensitive customer data to third-party AI models unless you have approved that path in writing.`,
      `<strong>Maintenance.</strong> The first five months after launch are included free: content and price updates, bug fixes, dependency and security updates, backups, uptime and speed checks, and basic SEO health checks in Search Console. After that, maintenance starts from ${P.care}, or you contact us only when something needs changing. You can stop at any time and keep every credential. Full details are on the <a href="/pricing/">pricing page</a>.`,
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "ecommerce-us",
    heading: "Ecommerce for US customers: Stripe, Shopify, PayPal, Square and sales tax",
    paragraphs: [
      `Selling online in the United States looks simple from the outside and gets detailed quickly. Buyers expect card payments, Apple Pay and Google Pay on mobile, clear shipping costs before checkout, and a returns policy they can find. Behind the scenes, the store has to collect the right sales tax for the right addresses, send tidy order emails and hand orders to whatever ships them. An ecommerce store with us starts from ${P.shop}.`,
      `<strong>Shopify.</strong> The right default for most product brands. It is a managed platform, so hosting, security and PCI compliance for card data are handled by Shopify, and its app ecosystem covers reviews, subscriptions, bundles, loyalty and wholesale. We build custom themes that load quickly rather than stacking apps that each add a script. Shopify Payments, Shop Pay, PayPal and other wallets can all run on one checkout.`,
      `<strong>Stripe on a custom or WooCommerce build.</strong> Better when you need something Shopify makes awkward: subscriptions with usage-based pricing, quotes that turn into invoices, B2B accounts with net terms, marketplaces with split payouts, or a checkout embedded inside a web app. Stripe Checkout or Payment Elements keep card data off your server entirely, and Stripe Billing handles recurring plans, trials and dunning.`,
      `<strong>Square.</strong> The natural choice when you also sell in person, at a counter, a market stall or a pop-up, and want one inventory and one set of reports across online and offline sales. We connect Square Online or a custom storefront to your existing Square catalog.`,
      `<strong>PayPal and wallets.</strong> PayPal is best added as an additional option rather than the only one; some customers trust it more than typing a card number into an unfamiliar store. Apple Pay and Google Pay are usually a setting away on Shopify and Stripe and noticeably shorten mobile checkout.`,
      `<strong>Sales tax.</strong> Since the Supreme Court's 2018 decision in South Dakota v. Wayfair, states can require online sellers to collect sales tax once they pass that state's economic nexus threshold, even without a physical presence there. Rates and rules vary by state, county and city, a handful of states have no statewide sales tax, and some products are taxed differently from others. We treat all of this as configuration: Stripe Tax, Shopify Tax, TaxJar or Avalara set to collect in the states where you are registered, with tax shown on the cart, receipt and order email. Where you need to register, and how your products are classified, is a decision for you and your accountant. We do not give tax advice.`,
      `<strong>Shipping and fulfillment.</strong> We set up rates by zone, weight or flat tiers, live carrier rates from USPS, UPS or FedEx where your plan supports them, free-shipping thresholds, local pickup, and label printing through Shopify Shipping, ShipStation or your 3PL. Order data can flow to your warehouse system, and tracking links reach the customer automatically.`,
      `<strong>Marketing plumbing.</strong> Product feeds for Google Merchant Center, Meta catalog sync, Klaviyo or Mailchimp flows for abandoned carts and post-purchase emails, and server-side conversion tracking where it improves accuracy. Marketing sign-ups at checkout use an unticked box, and every marketing email carries an unsubscribe link, in line with CAN-SPAM.`,
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "seo-us",
    heading: "SEO for Google in the United States: national, local and multilingual",
    paragraphs: [
      `US search is competitive and slow to move, which makes shortcuts expensive. Our approach is deliberately unglamorous: fix the technical foundation, build pages that genuinely answer what buyers search for, and earn relevance rather than buy it. Every site we build ships with clean URLs, unique titles and descriptions, structured data, an XML sitemap, sensible internal linking and fast mobile pages that pass Core Web Vitals.`,
      `<strong>National SEO.</strong> A business that sells across the country, such as a SaaS product, an online store or a B2B supplier, should not create a page for every city just because it can. Those pages end up thin and compete with each other. National sites do better with strong category, product and use-case pages, honest comparison and pricing content, and articles that earn links because they are useful. For B2B and SaaS, that often means pages for each integration, industry and job to be done.`,
      `<strong>Local SEO.</strong> A business with a physical location or service area wins or loses in the map results. That means a fully completed Google Business Profile, owned and verified by you, with the most specific primary category, accurate hours, services and real photographs; consistent name, address and phone across the directories that feed local results, such as Apple Business Connect, Bing Places, Yelp, the BBB and industry-specific listings; LocalBusiness schema; review requests built into your follow-up process; and one location or service-area page per real market, each with details a visitor would actually find useful, such as neighborhoods covered, typical jobs, local permits or response times. We never create fake locations or buy reviews, which also protects you from profile suspensions.`,
      `<strong>Spanish-language SEO.</strong> Tens of millions of people in the US speak Spanish at home, and in markets such as Miami, Houston, San Antonio, Los Angeles, Phoenix and much of the Southwest, a real Spanish section can reach customers competitors ignore. We build it as its own set of URLs with en-US and es-US hreflang pairs, translated titles, descriptions and structured data, and keyword research done in Spanish rather than translated from English, because people search differently in each language.`,
      `<strong>Selling into Canada too.</strong> If you ship to Canada, we can add en-CA pages where prices, shipping or regulations genuinely differ, with hreflang that tells Google which version is meant for whom and an x-default fallback. If the pages would be nearly identical, we will usually recommend keeping one version rather than creating near-duplicates. See our <a href="/canada/">Canada page</a> for how we approach that market.`,
      `<strong>AI search and answer engines.</strong> More US buyers now meet brands through AI Overviews in Google, ChatGPT search and Perplexity. The fundamentals that help there are the same ones that help classic SEO: clear, factual pages that answer specific questions, visible authorship and business details, structured data, and crawlable HTML rather than content hidden behind scripts. We build with that in mind and allow reputable AI crawlers unless you prefer otherwise.`,
      `<strong>Reporting.</strong> Monthly SEO starts from ${P.seo} and comes with a report built on Search Console and analytics data: impressions, clicks, queries gaining ground, technical fixes made and what is planned next. If a month was flat, the report says so. More detail is on the <a href="/services/seo-services/">SEO services page</a>.`,
    ],
