  {
    id: "compliance",
    heading: "Compliance: ADA and WCAG, CCPA and state privacy laws, CAN-SPAM, TCPA and sales tax",
    paragraphs: [
      `This section describes how we build, not legal advice. We are engineers, not lawyers. We do not certify compliance with any law, and we recommend that your attorney reviews your policies and anything that depends on how a law applies to your business. What we can do is build the site so that review has very little left to fix.`,
      `<strong>ADA and WCAG accessibility.</strong> US businesses receive a steady volume of website accessibility demand letters and lawsuits, most citing the Americans with Disabilities Act and pointing to WCAG as the practical yardstick. For state and local government websites, the Department of Justice adopted WCAG 2.1 AA as the technical standard under Title II in 2024, and many private organizations use the same benchmark. Our default practice follows WCAG 2.1 AA: semantic HTML with proper headings and landmarks, full keyboard navigation, visible focus states, contrast that clears the required ratios, labeled and described form fields, meaningful alt text, captions or transcripts where you supply video, and no information conveyed by color alone. We test key templates with automated tools, a keyboard and a screen reader before launch.`,
      `We also avoid accessibility overlay widgets that promise instant compliance with a line of JavaScript. They have a poor record with real screen reader users and have not reliably prevented claims. Fixing the markup is slower and actually works. If accessibility is a priority, say so at scoping and we will add audit checkpoints to the schedule rather than testing only at the end.`,
      `<strong>CCPA and CPRA in California.</strong> If your business meets the thresholds of the California Consumer Privacy Act, as amended by the CPRA, your site generally needs a notice at collection wherever personal information is gathered, a privacy policy describing your practices, and, if you sell or share personal information, including through some advertising and tracking tags, a "Do Not Sell or Share My Personal Information" link. California's regulations also expect businesses to treat a browser's Global Privacy Control signal as a valid opt-out. On the build side we load advertising and analytics tags through a consent layer, detect GPC and suppress the relevant tags when it is present, place notices beside forms, and set up a request route for access and deletion requests.`,
      `<strong>Other state privacy laws.</strong> California is no longer alone. More than a dozen states, including Virginia, Colorado, Connecticut, Utah, Texas and Oregon, have passed comprehensive consumer privacy laws, each with its own thresholds, and more keep arriving. The practical pattern for websites is similar: say what you collect, offer a way to opt out of targeted advertising and data sales, and honor recognized opt-out signals where required. We build sites so these controls can be switched on per state or nationwide without a redesign.`,
      `<strong>Health information.</strong> Clinics and health businesses need extra care. If a project touches protected health information under HIPAA, we will discuss hosting, vendors and business associate agreements with you, keep general web forms from collecting health details, and connect to your practice software rather than storing records ourselves. Your compliance lead should sign off; we do not certify HIPAA compliance.`,
      `<strong>CAN-SPAM for marketing email.</strong> Newsletters and promotional email sent from the forms and automations we build should follow CAN-SPAM: accurate sender and subject lines, your valid physical postal address in each message, and a working unsubscribe that is honored promptly. We connect forms to platforms such as Mailchimp, Klaviyo or HubSpot that handle unsubscribes properly, and never add people to a marketing list from a contact form without a clear opt-in.`,
      `<strong>TCPA and SMS.</strong> Text message marketing carries more risk in the US than email. The Telephone Consumer Protection Act and carrier rules generally require prior express written consent for marketing texts, and business texting over US carriers usually requires registering your brand and campaign. When a project includes SMS, we build a separate, unticked consent checkbox with clear wording, store when and how consent was given, and support STOP replies. Confirm your consent language with counsel before launch.`,
      `<strong>Sales tax.</strong> As covered in the ecommerce section, we configure tax collection for the states you are registered in; your accountant decides where that is.`,
